1. THREE LAMBDAS, ONE VARIABLE
     fs = [lambda: i for i in range(3)]
     [f() for f in fs]                            [2, 2, 2]          all three read i when called, and i ended at 2

2. THE LOOKUP HAPPENS WHEN THE FUNCTION RUNS
     x = 1
     def f(): return x
     x = 2
     f()                                          2                  f reads x now, not when def ran
     del x
     f()                                          NameError          and if x is gone by then, it fails now

3. WHAT A CLOSURE HOLDS: A CELL, WHICH CAN BE SHARED
     def counter():
         n = 0
         def inc():
             nonlocal n
             n += 1
             return n
         def get():
             return n
         return inc, get
     inc, get = counter()
     inc(), inc(), get()                          (1, 2, 2)          get sees what inc did: one cell
     inc.__closure__[0] is get.__closure__[0]     True
     get.__closure__[0].cell_contents             2                  the cell holds the current value

4. THREE WAYS TO CAPTURE THE VALUE INSTEAD
     fs = [lambda i=i: i for i in range(3)]
     [f() for f in fs]                            [0, 1, 2]          a default is computed once, when the lambda is made
     fs = [functools.partial(lambda i: i, i) for i in range(3)]
     [f() for f in fs]                            [0, 1, 2]          partial stores the argument now
     def keep(i): return lambda: i
     fs = [keep(i) for i in range(3)]
     [f() for f in fs]                            [0, 1, 2]          each call to keep is a new scope, so a new cell

5. THE LOOP VARIABLE OUTLIVES THE LOOP
     for k in range(3): pass
     k                                            2                  a for loop makes no scope of its own
     squares = [k * k for k in range(10)]
     k                                            2                  a comprehension does: its k never leaked

6. AND ASSIGNING TO THE NAME INSIDE MAKES IT LOCAL
     total = 0
     def bump(): total += 1
     bump()                                       UnboundLocalError  the += made total local to bump, so the read finds nothing
     def bump():
         global total
         total += 1
     bump()
     total                                        1
